#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106903} :activity/id #uuid "70093123-9441-504b-9d19-1ff886bae1a9" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107157 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given a point $P$ with position vector $\\mathbf{p},$ and another vector $\\mathbf{v}.$ \n\n![](images/b1/b1bb8d0cbe5dcbb24e224e8efc77e32db7a1657b245666aebd7b18107ee41149.png)\n\nThe **vector equation** of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ and passes through $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=p+tv,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{v}$ is often called the **direction vector** of the line, while the vector $\\mathbf{r}$ denotes the position vector of an arbitrary point on the line.\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{p}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe vector equation of the line  that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ and passes through $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5bf1db5d-9d49-578a-b4a3-de51006d5ffd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7711 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "da72d9c9-d225-5f92-acf1-85ac68f4a4ea" :step/math-academy-id 44724 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106904}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105896 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcf425bc-d6ce-5cab-87ea-2d4d42270d09"} :step/id #uuid "cec448b9-8490-5eef-b44a-d69c60421dab" :step/math-academy-id 44725 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106905}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105897 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6dee200-f517-5645-87ec-ccf36012e55e"} :step/id #uuid "a9e901bc-5996-547e-9050-8a27e0a73211" :step/math-academy-id 44728 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106906}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106906 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107158 :tutorial/content "There is a geometric intuition behind the formula $\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v},$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/93/93b062a23c613184e7cdf725852401168b609a32056a5efcd24ceca2703d81d2.png)\n\nTo get to any point $A(x,y,z)$ on the desired line, starting from the origin $O,$ we can\n\n- first travel along the vector $\\mathbf{p}$ to get to the point $P$ on the line, and\n\n- then travel along the vector $t \\mathbf{v}$ to get to $A.$ (The scalar $t$ represents how far we travel forward or backward along the line in the direction of $\\mathbf{v}.$)" :tutorial/id #uuid "24f7081a-5ffa-545e-9268-8abff5267044" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7712 :tutorial/title "Geometric Intuition Behind the Formula"} :step/id #uuid "1882d34e-68eb-5861-b83e-9a6271a116a2" :step/math-academy-id 44731 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106907}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106907 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105898 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c338d1eb-7e13-5089-8dd3-d5ca6eefd4ba"} :step/id #uuid "7ec783db-e49b-5e4a-a359-af68f2d97438" :step/math-academy-id 44732 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106909}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106908 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105899 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f78fe749-e37e-53f2-879f-4c36931c1d6f"} :step/id #uuid "32a3a6c0-a3b3-531d-881f-278b4030a495" :step/math-academy-id 44735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106909 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107159 :tutorial/content "![](images/44/443bbe1d201f1919fa2fb7ce3b632715cdc0e8dc77ff94bdc89f2cff7402b772.png)\n\nSuppose we wish to find a vector equation of the line *segment* whose endpoints are $P(3,-1,7)$ and $Q(3,4,4).$ \n\nTo do this, we need to find the vector equation of the corresponding line and then restrict the domain of the parameter $t.$\n\nFirst, we need to find a direction vector parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PQ}$ is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n=\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5t-1 \\\\ 7-3t\\end{bmatrix}, \\qquad t\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$\n\nThis equation describes every point on the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PQ}.$ \n\nTo restrict this to points on the segment $\\overline{PQ}$ only, we set $t\\in [0,1].$ Thus, the vector equation of the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) =\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5t-1 \\\\ 7-3t\\end{bmatrix},\n\\qquad t \\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nWriting the equation of a line segment in this way is sometimes called **parametrizing** the line segment since each point on the segment corresponds to a single parameter $t.$\n\n- Notice that $t=0$ gives us the position vector of $P{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(0) =\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5(0)-1 \\\\ 7-3(0)\\end{bmatrix}\n=\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} = \\mathbf p\n$$\n\n- Similarly, $t=1$ gives us the position vector of $Q{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(1) =\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5(1)-1 \\\\ 7-3(1)\\end{bmatrix}\n=\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}  = \\mathbf q\n$$\n\nSince increasing $t$ moves our vector $\\mathbf r (t)$ from $P$ to $Q,$ we say that the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is **traversed** *from* the point $P$ *to* the point $Q$ under this parametrization." :tutorial/id #uuid "5ec7269d-cea3-5a82-818b-cb048217f704" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7713 :tutorial/title "The Vector Equation of a Segment"} :step/id #uuid "50416523-59b6-572d-9328-3ca6a25a4c94" :step/math-academy-id 44736 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106908}}] :activity/title "The Vector Equation of a Line" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186106003}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431711} :activity/id #uuid "183eba74-280d-596d-84f9-614c47172a41" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431710 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431707 :question/id #uuid "5536a355-6822-5a47-a0a6-be384dfb79e8"} :step/id #uuid "385f8efa-dead-57c8-bb0d-ca58862d7bb2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431713}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431711 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431709 :question/id #uuid "cf9c9d04-5ab5-558b-bf38-eae2eb3b3fea"} :step/id #uuid "62732027-4291-5ae0-9766-6825a84c1896" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431712}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431712 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431708 :question/id #uuid "c91c661d-e879-5f90-a5fb-ceb93bf19954"} :step/id #uuid "6b10edff-9a52-5f78-b02b-67d728b26c18" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431710}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431713 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431706 :question/id #uuid "194bde74-7427-5189-95d5-f403b6acd3c5"} :step/id #uuid "90b2f552-3c11-524e-897e-0564ec54b233" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431714}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431714 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431705 :question/id #uuid "09c49afc-6085-50e6-863a-8dee0d62b478"} :step/id #uuid "983d1bf0-8aa7-5089-8bb5-ce39a8d426ba"}] :activity/title "The Vector Equation of a Line" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186431674}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492105} :activity/id #uuid "cdd38161-b7a8-5f07-98fd-ab57d6d2c3c9" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492082 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492050 :question/id #uuid "ba6720d7-134d-5884-b22f-7f4012ec3a77"} :step/id #uuid "1ec7ec1f-8794-50d0-a0c9-c532caebad39" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492093}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492089 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492072 :question